WEBVTT

00:00:41.130 --> 00:00:56.010
<v Ira Glass>Back during the rise of the dot coms and the internet, Jeff Kelling and a few of his friends were working as programmers together at this company in Dallas. And they decided they wanted to get together the way the tech geeks were doing all over the world at the time and come up with an idea for their own internet company to start.
<v Ira Glass>在互联网和网络热潮兴起时，Jeff Kelling和他的几个朋友在达拉斯一家公司担任程序员。当时世界各地的科技极客们都在这样做，他们也决定聚在一起，构思一个属于自己的互联网公司创业点子。

00:00:56.010 --> 00:01:11.680
<v Jeff Kelling>One of my business partners, Andy, his wife had just had a baby, and we started thinking about photo sharing. Andy could share his photos of his new baby with the grandparents that live across the state.
<v Jeff Kelling>我的商业合伙人之一Andy刚有了孩子，我们开始考虑照片共享。Andy可以将他宝宝的照片分享给住在州另一头的祖父母。

00:01:11.680 --> 00:01:22.860
<v Ira Glass>And this is 1999. This is before Flickr, this is before Shutterfly. But Jeff and his friends, they were not the first people to try to make a business out of photo sharing. There were other companies out there trying to make a go of that.
<v Ira Glass>那是1999年。在Flickr和Shutterfly出现之前。但Jeff和他的朋友们并不是第一批尝试做照片共享生意的人。当时还有其他公司也在尝试。

00:01:22.860 --> 00:01:45.790
<v Ira Glass>And it is not easy. It took Jeff and his partners years-- working nights, working weekends-- until finally in 2006, their startup was doing well enough that they could all quit their day jobs and do that full-time. Their company was called FotoTime-- that's FotoTime with an F. Just as they were living the dream, entrepreneurship, their own business, until--
<v Ira Glass>这并不容易。Jeff和他的合伙人们花了数年时间——利用夜晚和周末工作——直到2006年，他们的初创公司终于做得有声有色，他们可以辞掉日常工作，全职投入其中。他们的公司叫FotoTime——带F的FotoTime。就在他们实现梦想，经营着自己的事业时，直到——

00:01:45.790 --> 00:01:59.840
<v Jeff Kelling>We got a letter in May of 2008. And it wasn't a friendly letter. I mean, if you take a letter from the IRS that says, we're going to audit you, this letter was even less friendly than that.
<v Jeff Kelling>我们在2008年5月收到了一封信。那可不是一封友好的信。如果你收到国税局（IRS）说要审计你的信，这封信比那还要不友好得多。

00:01:59.840 --> 00:02:19.040
<v Jeff Kelling>It pretty much said, you're in violation of three of the patents that our company holds. You must contact us immediately to arrange payment and settlement, or we will be taking you to court.
<v Jeff Kelling>信中大意是说，你们侵犯了我们公司持有的三项专利。你们必须立即联系我们安排付款和和解，否则我们将把你们告上法庭。

00:02:19.040 --> 00:02:42.180
<v Jeff Kelling>And we were wondering, what is all this? I mean, this whole thing was developed internally. It's not like we went through the patent office and stole people's ideas. And it's also not like we were the first to do this. And we looked up this lawsuit online and we saw there were over 130 companies named in this lawsuit.
<v Jeff Kelling>我们当时很困惑，这到底是怎么回事？毕竟，整个项目都是内部开发的。我们又没去专利局窃取别人的想法。而且我们也不是第一个做这个的。我们在网上搜索了这个诉讼，发现有超过130家公司被列入其中。

00:02:42.180 --> 00:02:49.850
<v Ira Glass>All the big names were there-- Yahoo, which owns Flickr, as well as Shutterfly and Photobucket, and lots of small companies like Jeff's as well.
<v Ira Glass>所有大牌公司都在名单上——拥有Flickr的雅虎，以及Shutterfly和Photobucket，当然还有像Jeff公司那样的小公司。

00:02:49.850 --> 00:03:16.110
<v Ira Glass>The company suing Jeff was called FotoMedia. Jeff was FotoTime with an F, this was FotoMedia with an F. And one thing that was odd, they weren't actually a competitor of Jeff's. They didn't have a website where you could upload or share photos. And it wasn't clear to Jeff what in the world they thought he'd stolen from them. Had he accidentally come up with a way to upload files, or maybe see photos online, or do the credit card transactions that already somebody had some sort of patent on?
<v Ira Glass>起诉Jeff的公司叫FotoMedia。Jeff的公司是带F的FotoTime，这是带F的FotoMedia。奇怪的是，他们实际上并不是Jeff的竞争对手。他们根本没有网站让你上传或分享照片。Jeff完全搞不懂他们认为他到底窃取了什么。难道他意外发明了一种上传文件的方法，或者是在线查看照片，还是进行信用卡交易的方法，而这些已经有人申请了专利？

00:03:16.110 --> 00:03:28.160
<v Ira Glass>Or was it that he hadn't stolen anything? They were just looking for a payout, scamming him for some money? And what was especially galling to Jeff, when he called them to ask, what am I stealing from you guys? They wouldn't tell him.
<v Ira Glass>还是说他根本什么都没偷？他们只是想找人索要赔偿，骗取钱财？让Jeff特别愤怒的是，当他打电话询问他们，他到底从他们那儿偷了什么时，他们竟然拒绝告诉他。

00:03:28.160 --> 00:03:42.280
<v Jeff Kelling>That was a question they wouldn't answer. They said they wouldn't answer that until we got into court. So they wouldn't even identify what parts of our business or what they thought we were doing to use their technology.
<v Jeff Kelling>那是他们拒绝回答的一个问题。他们说在对簿公堂之前不会回答。所以他们甚至不肯指明我们业务的哪些部分，或者他们认为我们利用了他们的哪项技术。

00:03:42.280 --> 00:03:46.530
<v Ira Glass>But to go to court to answer that question was going to cost money, a lot of money.
<v Ira Glass>但为了在法庭上回答这个问题，需要花费很多钱，一大笔钱。

00:03:46.530 --> 00:04:01.220
<v Jeff Kelling>It was between $2 and $5 million. That's more than our company could handle, honestly. We knew we had to settle this thing somehow. There would be no more FotoTime today if we had to do that.
<v Jeff Kelling>金额在200万到500万美元之间。说实话，这超出了我们公司的承受能力。我们知道必须以某种方式解决这件事。如果我们不得不那样做，今天就不会再有FotoTime了。

00:04:01.220 --> 00:04:21.870
<v Ira Glass>Amazingly, it wasn't just Jeff and the other companies getting sued over these three patents who were upset. Out there in the world was an inventor who came up with the original ideas that got patented for two of these three patents, and he wasn't that happy about the lawsuit either. The guy's name was David Rose and he was issued these patents back when he started his own photo-sharing company back in the mid '90s, just a couple years before Jeff Kelling started his.
<v Ira Glass>令人惊讶的是，不仅是Jeff和其他因这三项专利被起诉的公司感到不满。世界上还有一位发明家，他提出了这三项专利中其中两项的原始构想，他对这场诉讼也不太高兴。这个人的名字叫David Rose，他在90年代中期创办自己的照片共享公司时获得了这些专利，比Jeff Kelling创业早了几年。

00:04:21.870 --> 00:04:32.950
<v Ira Glass>And he had gotten the patents in part because having a patent was just one of those things you did to raise money from investors, a check box you checked to prove you were serious and to protect yourself from some company swooping in and stealing your ideas.
<v Ira Glass>他获得这些专利的部分原因仅仅是因为申请专利是吸引投资者融资的手段之一，就像一个必须勾选的清单，用以证明你是认真的，并保护自己免受某些公司突然闯入并窃取你的想法。

00:04:32.950 --> 00:04:49.460
<v Ira Glass>He sold his company in 2000 and the patents that he got along with it. He thought the people who bought his company would expand the company and make it prosper. Hopefully, turn it into a household name. He has some problems with what they chose to do instead. He talked to This American Life producer Alex Blumberg and reporter Laura Sydell.
<v Ira Glass>他在2000年卖掉了公司，连同他获得的专利也一起卖了。他以为收购他公司的人会扩展业务并使其繁荣。希望将其打造成为家喻户晓的名字。对于他们实际所做的决定，他有一些不满。他向《This American Life》的制片人Alex Blumberg和记者Laura Sydell讲述了这一切。

00:04:49.460 --> 00:05:03.960
<v David Rose>It's the hoarding and non-operating of the technology that doesn't feel good. Because they didn't become the brand that they could have become. They had the protection. They could have built Flickr.
<v David Rose>那种囤积技术而不去运营的做法让人感觉很不好。因为他们并没有成为原本可以成为的那个品牌。他们拥有专利保护。他们本可以建立起Flickr。

00:05:03.960 --> 00:05:07.890
<v Alex Blumberg>Right. And instead they waited for somebody else to build Flickr, and then they sued Flickr?
<v Alex Blumberg>没错。结果他们却等着别人建立Flickr，然后去起诉Flickr？

00:05:07.890 --> 00:05:09.160
<v David Rose>Yes.
<v David Rose>是的。

00:05:09.160 --> 00:05:24.830
<v Ira Glass>Companies that make no products, but go around suing other companies that do make products over supposed patent infringement are so common in Silicon Valley these days that there's a derogatory term for it-- trolls, or patent trolls. David Rose explains, trolls--
<v Ira Glass>在硅谷，那些不生产产品，却四处起诉其他制造产品的公司声称其专利侵权的现象，如今已变得非常普遍，甚至有一个贬义词来形容他们——专利流氓（Patent Trolls）。David Rose解释道，流氓们——

00:05:24.830 --> 00:05:35.460
<v David Rose>You don't know that there's one under the bridge. They pop up. They have unreasonable demands. They can charge monopoly tolls or monopoly rents.
<v David Rose>你根本不知道桥下藏着一个。他们突然冒出来。提出不合理的要求。他们可以收取垄断性的过路费或垄断租金。

00:05:35.460 --> 00:05:43.430
<v Ira Glass>So the guy who came up with two of these patents doesn't want the lawsuit and the guy getting sued for the patents doesn't want the lawsuit. And yet, the lawsuit happens.
<v Ira Glass>所以，那个提出其中两项专利的发明家不想要这场诉讼，那个因专利被起诉的人也不想要这场诉讼。然而，诉讼还是发生了。

00:05:43.430 --> 00:06:02.020
<v Ira Glass>FotoMedia, by the way, denies being a patent troll. Its CEO told us that patent troll is a term that people throw around very loosely when they're in litigation over patent rights. As far as we can tell, and not a lot of this information is public, most of the companies being sued ended up doing what Jeff did, and agreed to pay FotoMedia money. Some of them were put out of business.
<v Ira Glass>顺便说一句，FotoMedia否认自己是专利流氓。其首席执行官告诉我们，“专利流氓”这个词是人们在进行专利权诉讼时非常随意地抛出的标签。据我们所知，虽然这些信息大多不公开，但大多数被起诉的公司最终都像Jeff一样选择了妥协，同意支付FotoMedia钱。其中一些公司因此倒闭了。

00:06:02.020 --> 00:06:09.750
<v Ira Glass>Jeff felt like he had no choice but to settle. He told Laura and Alex that reaching a settlement ended up taking six months, a very rough six months.
<v Ira Glass>Jeff觉得除了和解别无选择。他告诉Laura和Alex，达成和解的过程花了六个月，非常艰难的六个月。

00:06:09.750 --> 00:06:49.420
<v Jeff Kelling>It feels like if they're not reasonable, OK, our venture of 10 years is going to be gone. They have to be reasonable or we will just plain be gone. And we're talking about 10 years of our life. You know, honestly, as I'm talking about it now, it's kind of raising my heartbeat a little bit because I just remember how I personally felt. Just the huge amount of anxiety and lack of control over the whole situation. It was just an awful feeling.
<v Jeff Kelling>感觉如果他们不讲理，好了，我们经营了10年的事业就要化为乌有了。他们必须通情达理，否则我们就会彻底消失。这可是我们生命中的10年。老实说，我现在谈起这些时，心跳还会不由自主地加快，因为我清楚记得我当时个人的感受。那种巨大的焦虑和对全局失去掌控的感觉。那真的是一种糟糕透顶的感觉。

00:06:49.420 --> 00:06:53.380
<v Laura Sydell>Can I ask what that final settlement was, how much it was?
<v Laura Sydell>我能问一下最终的和解金额是多少吗？

00:06:53.380 --> 00:07:01.810
<v Jeff Kelling>No. No, unfortunately, part of the terms of our settlement agreement is that we don't discuss the amount.
<v Jeff Kelling>不行。不，遗憾的是，我们和解协议的条款之一就是不能透露具体金额。

00:07:01.810 --> 00:07:04.610
<v Alex Blumberg>Did it put your business in danger?
<v Alex Blumberg>这是否让你的企业陷入了困境？

00:07:04.610 --> 00:07:22.870
<v Jeff Kelling>It did, and they knew that. The settlement they wanted to get was just enough to put us in danger, but not to close us, and I'll stop there.
<v Jeff Kelling>确实如此，他们也知道这一点。他们想要达成的和解金额刚好足够让我们陷入困境，但不至于让我们倒闭，我只能说这么多。

00:07:22.870 --> 00:07:45.080
<v Ira Glass>Patents are so foundational to the American way of life that they're in the Constitution. Their purpose is quote "to promote the useful arts and sciences." In other words, to get people to share their ideas and inventions. To say to somebody like Eli Whitney, OK, you have this amazing invention, the cotton gin. If you tell everybody how it works and how to make their own, in exchange, any time somebody uses the idea, you get paid.
<v Ira Glass>专利是美国生活方式的基石，甚至被写进了宪法。它们的宗旨是“促进科学和工艺的进步”。换句话说，让人们分享他们的想法和发明。对像Eli Whitney这样的人说，好，你有了这个了不起的发明——轧棉机。如果你告诉大家它是如何运作以及如何制造的，作为交换，任何时候有人使用这个创意，你都会得到报酬。

00:07:45.080 --> 00:08:02.900
<v Ira Glass>If there were no such thing as a patent, Eli Whitney would have to keep his invention hidden in a dark room with no windows. Nobody would steal the idea, and then people would bring him their cotton, and he'd spit it out for them all processed on the other side. Instead of that, we had thousands of cotton gins everywhere. Patents make it safe to share and to innovate.
<v Ira Glass>如果没有专利这回事，Eli Whitney就不得不把他的发明藏在没有窗户的黑屋子里。没有人会偷这个创意，人们会把棉花带给他，他在另一头处理好后再吐出来。而不是像现在这样，到处都有成千上万台轧棉机。专利让分享和创新变得安全。

00:08:02.900 --> 00:08:52.290
<v Ira Glass>But today, lots of investors and innovators in Silicon Valley, maybe the majority would tell you, the patent system is doing the exact opposite of what it's supposed to. It's not promoting innovation, it's stifling it. Because patent lawsuits are on the rise. Patent trolls are on the move. Patent lawsuits are so common now that it's hard to find even one semi-successful startup in Silicon Valley that has not been hit with a suit. Which slows innovation, makes it harder for companies to prosper, hurts our global competitiveness-- is this getting big enough for you? Costs us all more money when we buy the stuff these companies sell. From WBEZ Chicago, it's This American Life distributed by Public Radio International. I'm Ira Glass. Today on our show, "When Patents Attack." Our guides this hour are going to be NPR correspondent Laura Sydell and This American Life producer and Planet Money co-host Alex Blumberg. OK, take it away, guys.
<v Ira Glass>但今天，硅谷的许多投资者和创新者，也许是大多数人会告诉你，专利制度正在做着与它初衷完全相反的事情。它不是在促进创新，而是在扼杀创新。因为专利诉讼呈上升趋势。专利流氓在行动。专利诉讼现在太普遍了，以至于在硅谷很难找到一家半成功且没被起诉过的初创公司。这减缓了创新，使公司更难繁荣，损害了我们的全球竞争力——你觉得这个影响够大了吧？这增加了我们购买这些公司产品时的成本。来自WBEZ芝加哥，这是由美国公共广播国际（PRI）发行的《This American Life》。我是Ira Glass。今天节目的话题是“当专利发起攻击时”。本期的向导是NPR记者Laura Sydell和《This American Life》制片人兼《Planet Money》联合主持人Alex Blumberg。好了，交给你们了。

00:08:52.290 --> 00:09:02.960
<v Laura Sydell>The term patent troll was first coined by a guy named Peter Detkin, who at the time was one of the top lawyers at one of the biggest tech companies in the world, the computer chip maker Intel.
<v Laura Sydell>“专利流氓”这个词最早由一个叫Peter Detkin的人提出，他当时是全球最大科技公司之一——芯片制造商英特尔的顶级律师。

00:09:02.960 --> 00:09:12.180
<v Alex Blumberg>Around 1999, Intel found itself in the position that Jeff Kelling-- the guy we heard from at the top of the show-- was in. Getting approached by a company that didn't build anything.
<v Alex Blumberg>大约在1999年，英特尔发现自己处于和Jeff Kelling——我们在节目开头听到的那个人——一样的境地。一家什么都不生产的公司找上门来。

00:09:12.180 --> 00:09:20.760
<v Peter Detkin>Simply saying, I have a patent that covers semiconductors generally. You make a semiconductor, and therefore, you should pay me some money. And there were a lot of claims like that.
<v Peter Detkin>简单地说，我有一项涵盖半导体的通用专利。你制造了半导体，因此你应该付我钱。当时有很多这样的索赔。

00:09:20.760 --> 00:09:34.790
<v Laura Sydell>One lawsuit in particular made Peter Detkin so mad that he called the lawyer who filed it a quote "patent extortionist." The lawyer turned around and sued Peter Detkin for libel. So Detkin needed a better name.
<v Laura Sydell>特别是一起诉讼让Peter Detkin非常生气，以至于他称提起该诉讼的律师为“专利敲诈者”。那位律师转过身来起诉Peter Detkin诽谤。所以Detkin需要一个更好的名字。

00:09:34.790 --> 00:10:07.620
<v Peter Detkin>So I had a contest inside Intel. The contest itself was named "The Terrorist." And these suggestions-- we got a lot of suggestions, but none really fit. But at the time my daughter was, I think, four or five and she liked playing with those little troll dolls. The original one, in fact, is still in my office. And so I turned to her and I said, oh, the story of a troll kind of fits because the whole Billy Goats Gruff thing. It's someone lying under a bridge they didn't build, demanding payment from anybody who passed. I said, how about a patent troll?
<v Peter Detkin>我在英特尔内部举办了一场竞赛。竞赛本身就叫“恐怖分子”。我们收到了很多建议，但没有一个真正合适。但在当时，我女儿大概四五岁，她喜欢玩那些小精灵玩偶（troll dolls）。事实上，最初的那个至今还在我的办公室里。所以我转向她说，哦，“流氓”（troll）的故事很贴切，就像那段《三只公山羊格拉夫》的故事。那是一个人躺在并非他建造的桥下，要求过路人付钱。我说，叫“专利流氓”怎么样？

00:10:07.620 --> 00:10:21.340
<v Alex Blumberg>The name stuck, and if anything, the problem of patent trolls just got worse. From 2004 to 2009, the number of patent infringement lawsuits jumped by 70%. Licensing fee requests, like what happened to Peter Detkin and to FotoTime, went up 650%.
<v Alex Blumberg>这个名字传开了，而且如果说有什么变化的话，专利流氓的问题只会变得更严重。从2004年到2009年，专利侵权诉讼的数量猛增了70%。像Peter Detkin和FotoTime那样遇到的许可费要求增加了650%。

00:10:21.340 --> 00:10:27.560
<v Alex Blumberg>Today, pretty much any time you talk to a computer or tech person in Silicon Valley and the subject of patents comes up, everyone groans.
<v Alex Blumberg>今天，在硅谷几乎任何时候你和电脑或科技人士谈论专利话题，每个人都会发出呻吟声。

00:10:27.560 --> 00:10:42.030
<v Laura Sydell>I hear those groans a lot. I live out here in San Francisco, and just to get a flavor of this, the other day I went down to a park called South Park around noon. It's a place where a lot of tech workers eat their lunch. And the sentiment is universal.
<v Laura Sydell>我经常听到这种呻吟声。我住在旧金山，为了感受一下，前几天中午我去了南公园（South Park）。那是许多科技工作者吃午餐的地方。这种情绪是普遍的。

00:10:42.030 --> 00:10:45.070
<v Laura Sydell>If I say patents system, what do you say?
<v Laura Sydell>如果我说“专利制度”，你会怎么说？

00:10:45.070 --> 00:10:51.240
<v Man 1>I think it's just a way for lawyers to make money and basically, it's a killer for creativity.
<v Man 1>我觉得这只是律师赚钱的一种方式，从根本上说，它是创意的终结者。

00:10:51.240 --> 00:10:54.770
<v Man 2>Complicated, broken.
<v Man 2>复杂，破碎。

00:10:54.770 --> 00:10:59.810
<v Man 3>It's basically a flimflam game that anybody who knows how to take advantage of it is doing.
<v Man 3>这基本上就是一场骗局，任何知道如何利用它的人都在做。

00:10:59.810 --> 00:11:02.090
<v Laura Sydell>Do you kind of groan when you hear the word?
<v Laura Sydell>听到这个词时，你会呻吟吗？

00:11:02.090 --> 00:11:04.150
<v Man 4>I do, yeah. You want to hear a groan for the radio?
<v Man 4>会，是的。你想听听电台里的呻吟声吗？

00:11:04.150 --> 00:11:05.210
<v Laura Sydell>Yeah, let's hear a groan.
<v Laura Sydell>好，让我们听听。

00:11:05.210 --> 00:11:12.000
<v Man 4>Grrrrr.
<v Man 4>呃……

00:11:12.000 --> 00:11:16.800
<v Laura Sydell>If I say the word patent troll, does any company or any entity come to mind in particular?
<v Laura Sydell>如果我说“专利流氓”这个词，有没有哪家公司或实体特别让你想到？

00:11:16.800 --> 00:11:28.750
<v Man 5>Like Nathan Myhrvold, I guess, and whatever his company is. It has some stupid name, like Associate Associates or something like that, I don't know.
<v Man 5>比如Nathan Myhrvold，我想，还有他的公司。名字很蠢，像“联合联合（Associate Associates）”之类的，我不知道。

