<v Laura Sydell>David Martin说，当他第一次开始研究这些东西，看到所有这些本质上是相同的东西却被授予了专利时——

00:21:52.140 --> 00:22:14.580
<v David Martin>We thought that would be an anomaly. And then we were told, oh no, it's not an anomaly. That happens. So that's what got us into the rabbit hole you're about to see. Which is to say, well, let's see how many times that happens. And as I've testified in Congress, that happens about 30% of the time in US patents.
<v David Martin>我们以为那只是个例外。然后有人告诉我们，哦不，那不是例外。那是常态。这就是让我们掉进你即将看到的那个兔子洞的原因。也就是说，好吧，让我们看看这种情况发生过多少次。正如我在国会作证时所说的那样，这种情况在美国专利中大约占30%。

00:22:14.580 --> 00:22:19.620
<v Alex Blumberg>That is, 30% of US patents are essentially for things that have already been invented.
<v Alex Blumberg>也就是说，30%的美国专利本质上都是针对已经发明出来的东西。

00:22:19.620 --> 00:22:26.080
<v David Martin>So, for example, toast becomes the thermal refreshening of a bread product.
<v David Martin>所以，举个例子，吐司面包变成了面包产品的热刷新。

00:22:26.080 --> 00:22:27.180
<v Laura Sydell>These are real patents?
<v Laura Sydell>这些是真实存在的专利吗？

00:22:27.180 --> 00:22:27.830
<v David Martin>Yes.
<v David Martin>是的。

00:22:27.830 --> 00:22:28.930
<v Alex Blumberg>There's a patent on toast?
<v Alex Blumberg>真的有关于吐司面包的专利？

00:22:28.930 --> 00:22:31.790
<v David Martin>Yes, thermally refreshened bread, not on toast.
<v David Martin>是的，热刷新面包，不是针对吐司。

00:22:31.790 --> 00:22:41.110
<v Laura Sydell>Ladies and gentlemen, patent number 6080436, bread refreshing method, issued in 2000.
<v Laura Sydell>女士们先生们，6080436号专利，面包刷新方法，2000年发布。

00:22:41.110 --> 00:22:47.340
<v Alex Blumberg>And we talked to another expert who told us Chris Crawford's patent was actually similar to the toast patent in one respect.
<v Alex Blumberg>我们与另一位专家交谈，他告诉我们Chris Crawford的专利在某些方面实际上与吐司专利相似。

00:22:47.340 --> 00:22:49.820
<v Rick Mcleod>None of this was actually new.
<v Rick Mcleod>这些其实都不是什么新东西。

00:22:49.820 --> 00:23:07.630
<v Alex Blumberg>Rick McLeod is a patent lawyer and former software engineer who we also asked to evaluate Chris Crawford's patent. So he went on a search. This is how patent lawyers research this kind of thing. He looked to see if anyone else in the field was already doing the thing Chris Crawford claimed to invent in 1993 when he first filed his patent.
<v Alex Blumberg>Rick McLeod是一位专利律师和前软件工程师，我们也请他评估了Chris Crawford的专利。所以他进行了一次搜索。这就是专利律师研究这类事情的方式。他查看在1993年Chris Crawford首次申请专利时，该领域是否还有其他人已经在做他声称发明的事情。

00:23:07.630 --> 00:23:29.720
<v Rick Mcleod>There were institutions, both academic and businesses, that used computers in this way. And I think it's a very interesting collection of things that were well known in the 1980s, with the exception of it adds on the word internet.
<v Rick Mcleod>当时确实有一些机构，无论是学术界还是商界，都在以这种方式使用电脑。我认为这是一系列在80年代就众所周知的有趣事情，唯一的区别就是增加了“互联网”这个词。

00:23:29.720 --> 00:23:33.900
<v Laura Sydell>Do you think this patent should have been issued in the first place?
<v Laura Sydell>你认为这项专利最初应该被批准吗？

00:23:33.900 --> 00:23:42.170
<v Rick Mcleod>No, I don't.
<v Rick Mcleod>不，我不这么认为。

00:23:42.170 --> 00:24:04.010
<v Alex Blumberg>And in fact, for a long time, the Patent Office would have agreed with Rick McLeod. The Patent Office used to be very reluctant to issue patents for software. For decades, it considered software to be like language. Software programs were more like books or articles. You could copyright them, but you couldn't patent them. They weren't inventions like the cotton gin.
<v Alex Blumberg>事实上，在很长一段时间里，专利局也会同意Rick McLeod的观点。专利局过去非常不情愿为软件颁发专利。几十年来，它认为软件就像语言。软件程序更像是书或文章。你可以拥有它们的版权，但不能为它们申请专利。它们不像轧棉机那样的发明。

00:24:04.010 --> 00:24:19.480
<v Laura Sydell>But the federal courts stepped in and they started chipping away at this interpretation. There was a big decision in 1994 and another one in 1998, which rejected the Patent Office's view. The Patent Office got the memo and a flood of software patents followed.
<v Laura Sydell>但联邦法院介入了，他们开始削弱这种解释。1994年有一个重大判决，1998年又有一个，驳回了专利局的观点。专利局收到了信息，随后涌现了大量的软件专利。

00:24:19.480 --> 00:24:27.770
<v Laura Sydell>A lot of people in Silicon Valley wish that that had never happened, including a very surprising group-- software engineers.
<v Laura Sydell>硅谷的许多人希望这一切从未发生过，包括一个非常令人惊讶的群体——软件工程师。

00:24:27.770 --> 00:24:36.050
<v Stephan Brunner>I have to say I actually worked on a whole bunch of patents in my career over the years and I have to say that every single patent is nothing but crap.
<v Stephan Brunner>我不得不说，我职业生涯中实际上处理过一大堆专利，我必须说，每一项专利除了废纸一张什么都不是。

00:24:36.050 --> 00:24:46.320
<v Laura Sydell>This is Stephan Brunner, a programmer. He said something we heard from a lot of software engineers. His software patents don't even make sense to him.
<v Laura Sydell>这是程序员Stephan Brunner。他说了一些我们从许多软件工程师那里听到的话。他的软件专利对他来说甚至毫无意义。

00:24:46.320 --> 00:25:16.970
<v Stephan Brunner>I can't tell you for the hell of it what they're actually supposed to do. Because the company said we have to do a patent on this, and then they send in a lawyer. You basically say, that's probably right. That's probably wrong. And they just write something up. It makes no sense. And personally when I look at them, I'm not proud at all because most of them again, it's just like mumbo jumbo, which nobody understands, and which makes no sense from an engineering standpoint whatsoever.
<v Stephan Brunner>我根本说不出它们到底应该做什么。因为公司说我们必须为这个申请专利，然后他们就派来一位律师。你基本就说，那大概是对的。那大概是错的。然后他们就写出一些东西。这毫无意义。我个人看它们的时候，一点都不感到自豪，因为它们中的大多数就像咒语一样，没人能听懂，而且从工程角度来看完全没有意义。

00:25:16.970 --> 00:25:41.080
<v Alex Blumberg>Stephan Brunner, patent 7650296 quote, "a configurator using structure and rules to provide a user interface." One sample section and trust me, the whole thing is like this. "According to one embodiment of the invention, a customizable product class is created. A component product class is added to the customizable product class where the component product class is a sub-class of the customizable product."
<v Alex Blumberg>Stephan Brunner，7650296号专利，关于“使用结构和规则来提供用户界面的配置器”。其中一个样本部分，请相信我，整个东西都像这样。“根据本发明的一个实施例，创建了一个可自定义的产品类。组件产品类被添加到可自定义的产品类中，其中组件产品类是可自定义类的子类。”

00:25:41.080 --> 00:26:00.060
<v Laura Sydell>In polls, as many as 80% of software engineers say the patent system actually hinders innovation. In other words, it does exactly the opposite of what it's supposed to do. It doesn't encourage them to come up with new ideas and create new products, it actually gets in their way. Here's another programmer, Adam Cohen.
<v Laura Sydell>在民意调查中，多达80%的软件工程师表示专利制度实际上阻碍了创新。换句话说，它做了与它预期完全相反的事情。它并没有鼓励他们提出新想法并创造新产品，反而妨碍了他们。这是另一位程序员Adam Cohen。

00:26:00.060 --> 00:26:24.640
<v Adam Cohen>I work for a company, which I'm not going to say, that at the end of the company we tried to patent-- we did successfully patent our software that we made. And that patent is really meaningless because everybody that has an internet website, basically almost today, uses the stuff that we patented to make their website work. Almost everybody.
<v Adam Cohen>我为一家公司工作，我不打算说出名字，我们在公司末期试图申请专利——我们确实成功地为我们制作的软件申请了专利。那个专利确实是毫无意义的，因为今天几乎每个拥有互联网网站的人，基本上都在使用我们申请了专利的那些东西来让他们的网站工作。几乎每个人。

00:26:24.640 --> 00:26:35.780
<v Alex Blumberg>This, we heard, happens all the time-- patents that are so broad, everyone's guilty of infringement. Which causes huge problems for almost anyone trying to start or grow a business on the internet.
<v Alex Blumberg>我们听说这种情况经常发生——专利太宽泛了，每个人都有侵权之嫌。这给几乎任何试图在互联网上创业或扩大业务的人带来了巨大问题。

00:26:35.780 --> 00:26:46.130
<v Chris Sacca>We're at a point in the state of intellectual property where existing patents probably cover every single behavior that's happening on the internet and our mobile phones today.
<v Chris Sacca>在知识产权现状方面，我们正处于这样一个点：现有的专利可能涵盖了互联网和移动电话上发生的每一个行为。

00:26:46.130 --> 00:26:52.900
<v Laura Sydell>This, again, is Chris Sacca, the investor you heard from earlier, who helped lots of companies, including Twitter, get off the ground.
<v Laura Sydell>这又是Chris Sacca，之前提到过的投资者，他帮助许多公司，包括Twitter，起步。

00:26:52.900 --> 00:27:14.030
<v Chris Sacca>So I have no doubt that the average Silicon Valley startup, or even medium-sized company, no matter how truly innovative they are, I have no doubt that aspects of whatever they're doing violate patents that are out there right now. And that's what's fundamentally broken about this system right now.
<v Chris Sacca>所以我毫无疑问，普通的硅谷初创公司，甚至中型公司，无论他们有多么创新，我毫无疑问，他们正在做的某些方面都违反了目前存在的专利。这就是该系统目前从根本上崩溃的原因。

00:27:14.030 --> 00:27:33.890
<v Alex Blumberg>And this brings us back to patent 5771354, Chris Crawford's patent, the patent Intellectual Ventures pointed us to as an example of how they encourage innovation. As we've said, this patent also seems to cover a big chunk of what's happening on the internet-- upgrading software, buying stuff online, what's called cloud storage, storing data on the internet.
<v Alex Blumberg>这把我们带回了5771354号专利，Chris Crawford的专利，这是Intellectual Ventures向我们展示作为他们如何鼓励创新例子的专利。如前所述，这项专利似乎也涵盖了互联网上正在发生的大部分事情——升级软件、在线购物、所谓的云存储、在互联网上存储数据。

00:27:33.890 --> 00:27:38.430
<v Alex Blumberg>If you have a patent on all that, you could sue a lot of people and make a lot of money.
<v Alex Blumberg>如果你拥有所有这些的专利，你可以起诉很多人并赚很多钱。

00:27:38.430 --> 00:28:01.650
<v Laura Sydell>And in fact, that's what's happening with Chris Crawford's patent. Intellectual Ventures sold it to another company, a company called Oasis Research, in June of 2010. Less than a month later, Oasis Research used the patent to sue 16 different tech companies-- companies like Rackspace, Go Daddy, and AT&T, companies that do cloud storage.
<v Laura Sydell>事实上，Chris Crawford的专利正在发生这种情况。Intellectual Ventures在2010年6月将其卖给了另一家公司，名为Oasis Research。不到一个月后，Oasis Research利用该专利起诉了16家不同的科技公司——像Rackspace、Go Daddy和AT&T这样的公司，那些做云存储的公司。

00:28:01.650 --> 00:28:05.630
<v Operator>You have reached Oasis Research. At the tone, please leave your name--
<v Operator>您已拨打Oasis Research。请在提示音后留下您的名字——

00:28:05.630 --> 00:28:28.180
<v Laura Sydell>I called the number on Oasis's website numerous times, but an actual human being never picked up. For a while, the message directed all questions to a lawyer in New York named John Desmarais. He also didn't return our phone calls, although I did track him down at a conference in San Francisco. I ran up to him right after a talk and asked him what he could tell me about Oasis Research.
<v Laura Sydell>我多次拨打了Oasis网站上的电话，但一直没有真人接听。有一段时间，留言指引所有问题询问纽约一位名叫John Desmarais的律师。他也一直没回我们的电话，尽管我确实在旧金山的一次会议上追踪到了他。我在他演讲结束后跑过去问他能告诉我们关于Oasis Research的什么信息。

00:28:28.180 --> 00:28:31.040
<v John Desmarais>I can't talk about folks I represent.
<v John Desmarais>我不能谈论我代理的人。

00:28:31.040 --> 00:28:33.790
<v Laura Sydell>Do you know who owns Oasis Research to reach them?
<v Laura Sydell>你知道谁拥有Oasis Research可以联系他们吗？

00:28:33.790 --> 00:28:38.880
<v John Desmarais>Yes, I do. Yes, but I'm not going to tell you.
<v John Desmarais>是的，我知道。是的，但我不会告诉你。

00:28:38.880 --> 00:28:47.310
<v Laura Sydell>I mean, they're bringing a suit and you literally can't tell us who owns the company?
<v Laura Sydell>我的意思是，他们正在发起诉讼，而你竟然不能告诉我们是谁拥有这家公司？

00:28:47.310 --> 00:28:50.630
<v John Desmarais>I'm not going to answer questions about pending lawsuits for you.
<v John Desmarais>我不会为你回答关于未决诉讼的问题。

00:28:50.630 --> 00:28:55.670
<v Laura Sydell>Another question. Is there any chance at a later date of actually talking to you more generally?
<v Laura Sydell>另一个问题。以后有没有可能和你进行更广泛的交流？

00:28:55.670 --> 00:29:08.020
<v John Desmarais>I don't think so, but thanks for asking. Although, I love NPR and I love the work that you guys do.
<v John Desmarais>我觉得不可能，但谢谢你的提问。虽然，我热爱NPR，也热爱你们所做的工作。

00:29:08.020 --> 00:29:26.470
<v Alex Blumberg>Behavior like this makes it hard not to think, are you hiding something? When someone says, yes, I know, but I'm not going to tell you, it really makes you want to find stuff out. There was hardly any public information about Oasis Research, minimal corporate filings. No way to know who owned it, how many employees it had, if it even had employees at all.
<v Alex Blumberg>这种行为让人很难不想，你们是不是在隐瞒什么？当有人说，是的，我知道，但我不会告诉你时，这真的会让你想找出真相。关于Oasis Research几乎没有任何公共信息，公司备案极少。没法知道谁拥有它，有多少员工，甚至是否真的有员工。

00:29:26.470 --> 00:29:34.160
<v Alex Blumberg>One of the few details that was available: an address, in Marshall, Texas. 104 East Houston Street, Suite 190.
<v Alex Blumberg>仅有的几个细节之一：一个地址，在德克萨斯州马歇尔市。东休斯顿街104号，190室。

00:29:34.160 --> 00:29:39.450
<v Michael Smith>Right now we're going into the first floor of the Baxter Building, which is 104 East Houston.
<v Michael Smith>现在我们正在进入Baxter大楼的一楼，地址是东休斯顿104号。

00:29:39.450 --> 00:30:01.020
<v Laura Sydell>This is Michael Smith. He's an attorney in Marshall, Texas, who does mostly patent cases. He agreed to show us the offices of Oasis Research. They're in a nondescript, two-story building on the town's main square, two doors down from the federal courthouse. He led us into a narrow corridor lined with doors with gold and black office name plates.
<v Laura Sydell>这是Michael Smith。他是德克萨斯州马歇尔市的一名律师，主要处理专利案件。他同意向我们展示Oasis Research的办公室。它们位于镇广场上一座不起眼的两层建筑里，距离联邦法院有两个门之遥。他把我们带进一条狭窄的走廊，两边排列着带有金色和黑色办公室门牌的门。

00:30:01.020 --> 00:30:04.990
<v Michael Smith>And here we go, suite 190, Oasis Research LLC.
<v Michael Smith>到了，190室，Oasis Research有限责任公司。

00:30:04.990 --> 00:30:12.710
<v Alex Blumberg>It was late morning on a weekday-- not a holiday-- but the door was locked. Through the crack underneath you could see there were no lights on inside.
<v Alex Blumberg>那天是工作日的深夜——不是节假日——但门锁着。透过门下的缝隙可以看到里面没开灯。

00:30:12.710 --> 00:30:19.450
<v Alex Blumberg>Marshall is a very small town, 24,000 people. Michael was born and raised here, so we started quizzing him about Oasis.
<v Alex Blumberg>马歇尔是一个很小的城镇，有24000人。Michael在这里出生长大，所以我们开始盘问他关于Oasis的事。

00:30:19.450 --> 00:30:23.410
<v Laura Sydell>Does it have any employees that you know about?
<v Laura Sydell>它有你知道的任何员工吗？

00:30:23.410 --> 00:30:25.330
<v Michael Smith>Not that I know of.
<v Michael Smith>据我所知没有。

00:30:25.330 --> 00:30:29.320
<v Laura Sydell>Have you ever seen any people coming in and out of that office?
<v Laura Sydell>你有没有见过有人进出那个办公室？

00:30:29.320 --> 00:30:33.470
<v Michael Smith>No, I haven't.
<v Michael Smith>没有，我没见过。

00:30:33.470 --> 00:30:36.020
<v Alex Blumberg>Is this office ever occupied?
<v Alex Blumberg>这个办公室有人办公吗？

00:30:36.020 --> 00:30:39.510
<v Michael Smith>I doubt that it is.
<v Michael Smith>我怀疑没有。

00:30:39.510 --> 00:30:46.400
<v Laura Sydell>If you don't mind, I'm going to knock on that door and just see if there's anyone here today.
<v Laura Sydell>如果你不介意的话，我想敲一下那扇门看看今天有没有人在。

00:30:46.400 --> 00:30:50.940
<v Alex Blumberg>I know this is kind of a cliche at this point, knocking on the door of the suspected fake office--
<v Alex Blumberg>我知道敲响疑似假办公室的门这在现在看来有点老套——

00:30:50.940 --> 00:30:51.410
<v Laura Sydell>Nothing.
<v Laura Sydell>什么反应也没有。

00:30:51.410 --> 00:30:53.500
<v Alex Blumberg>But we'd flown a long way.
<v Alex Blumberg>但我们飞了这么远。

00:30:53.500 --> 00:31:07.850
<v Laura Sydell>But I will say, standing in that corridor was eerie. All the other doors looked exactly the same-- locked, name plates over the door, no light coming out. It was a corridor of silent, empty offices.
<v Laura Sydell>但我得说，站在那条走廊里让人毛骨悚然。所有其他的门看起来都一模一样——锁着，门上有门牌，没有光线溢出。那是一条安静、空荡荡的办公走廊。

00:31:07.850 --> 00:31:22.870
<v Michael Smith>Right next door to Software Rights Archive, Bulletproof Technology of Texas, Jelly Fish Technology of Texas, and a couple others that I recognize as plaintiffs in cases that we're involved in here.
<v Michael Smith>隔壁就是Software Rights Archive，Bulletproof Technology of Texas，Jelly Fish Technology of Texas，还有几个我认出是我们这里参与案件的起诉方。

00:31:22.870 --> 00:31:28.080
<v Laura Sydell>Are there are a lot of companies like this here in East Texas?
<v Laura Sydell>像这样的公司在德克萨斯东部多吗？

00:31:28.080 --> 00:31:29.920
<v Michael Smith>Yes.
<v Michael Smith>很多。

00:31:29.920 --> 00:31:33.460
<v Alex Blumberg>And we're standing in the whole corridor of them, it seems like.
<v Alex Blumberg>而我们似乎正站在它们的走廊里。

00:31:33.460 --> 00:31:41.400
<v Michael Smith>Yes. This would be ground zero, yes.
<v Michael Smith>是的。这大概就是风暴中心了，没错。

00:31:41.400 --> 00:31:58.400
<v Laura Sydell>So what's going on here? It turns out a lot of those companies in that corridor, maybe every single one of them, are doing exactly what Oasis Research is doing. They appear to have no employees. They're not making new inventions here. They're filing lawsuits for patent infringement.
<v Laura Sydell>到底发生了什么？原来走廊里的许多公司，可能每一家都在做和Oasis Research一模一样的事。它们似乎没有任何员工。它们不是在这里搞什么新发明。它们是在提起专利侵权诉讼。

00:31:58.400 --> 00:32:04.950
<v Laura Sydell>Patent lawsuits, says Michael Smith, are big business in Marshall, part of the Eastern District of Texas.
<v Laura Sydell>Michael Smith说，专利诉讼在马歇尔是笔大生意，是德克萨斯东区的一部分。

00:32:04.950 --> 00:32:10.170
<v Laura Sydell>Walking back across the town square past the Eastern District Courthouse, Michael explained it.
<v Laura Sydell>走回镇广场经过东区法院时，Michael解释了这一点。

00:32:10.170 --> 00:32:23.820
<v Michael Smith>The Eastern District in the last few years has been either number one or number two or three in the nation in numbers of patent cases. The list of the patent cases at this courthouse would be about 2,000 cases long.
<v Michael Smith>过去几年东区在专利案件数量上一直是全国第一、第二或第三。这个法院的专利案件名单大约有2000件长。

00:32:23.820 --> 00:32:32.600
<v Laura Sydell>As we walked past the courthouse, as if to drive the point home, a gaggle of lawyers emerges-- dozens and dozens of dark suits in the hot Texas sun.
<v Laura Sydell>当我们经过法院时，仿佛为了加强这一点，一群律师出现了——几十个身穿深色西装的人出现在炎热的德克萨斯阳光下。

00:32:32.600 --> 00:32:40.120
<v Michael Smith>They're taking their mid-morning break during a patent trial that started yesterday morning in front of Magistrate Judge Everingham.
<v Michael Smith>他们正在昨天上午在Everingham地方法官面前开始的专利庭审中休息。

00:32:40.120 --> 00:32:51.540
