00:32:54.120 --> 00:33:02.220
<v Alex Blumberg>We talked to many people about why this is so. Why do all these New York and San Francisco-based companies come to tiny Marshall to battle in court?
<v Alex Blumberg>我们和许多人谈论过为什么会这样。为什么所有这些总部位于纽约和旧金山的公司都要来到小小的马歇尔在法庭上作战？

00:33:02.220 --> 00:33:09.700
<v Laura Sydell>Many people say it has to do with juries in Marshall. They are famously plaintiff friendly, friendly to patent owners trying to get a large verdict.
<v Laura Sydell>许多人说这与马歇尔的陪审团有关。他们以对原告友好而闻名，对试图获得大额赔偿的专利持有者很友好。

00:33:09.700 --> 00:33:16.860
<v Laura Sydell>But Michael Smith, who's argued on both sides of numerous patent cases, says that might have been true once, but not anymore.
<v Laura Sydell>但处理过无数专利案件双方的Michael Smith表示，那可能曾经是真的，但现在不是了。

00:33:16.860 --> 00:33:37.670
<v Alex Blumberg>He says they're in Marshall because of the drug war. Basically, in the '90s, federal courts everywhere were clogged with drug cases. Civil cases like patent cases couldn't get in front of a judge because criminal cases take precedence. So companies with patent suits had to find a spot with fewer criminals to prosecute-- hence, sleepy Marshall, Texas.
<v Alex Blumberg>他说他们之所以在马歇尔是因为禁毒战争。基本上，在90年代，各地的联邦法院都被毒品案件堵塞了。像专利案件这样的民事案件无法排期，因为刑事案件优先。所以有专利诉讼的公司必须找到一个刑事犯较少的地方——于是，沉睡的德克萨斯州马歇尔市就成了目标。

00:33:37.670 --> 00:34:08.370
<v Alex Blumberg>Our visit to Marshall made us realize, something big is going on here in Texas, and Oasis Research is part of it. 2,000 lawsuits making the same essential claim Intellectual Ventures makes. There's an inventor whose invention is being stolen, used without permission. But there were no inventors here, just corridors of empty offices and a lot of lawyers. It made us wonder, what else about Intellectual Ventures is not what it appears to be?
<v Alex Blumberg>我们对马歇尔的访问使我们意识到，德克萨斯州正在发生大事，而Oasis Research只是其中一部分。2000起诉讼提出了Intellectual Ventures所提出的同样的本质主张。这里有一位发明家，他的发明正在被窃取，在未经许可的情况下使用。但这里根本没有发明家，只有空荡荡的办公室走廊和一大堆律师。这让我们怀疑，Intellectual Ventures还有什么是虚有其表的？

00:34:08.370 --> 00:35:04.460
<v Ira Glass>Alex Blumberg and Laura Sydell. Coming up, we go back to Intellectual Ventures for answers, and we find out what a patent has in common with a weapon of mass destruction. That's in a minute, from Chicago Public Radio and Public Radio International, when our program continues.
