<v Alex Blumberg>So just to spell this out, Peter Detkin is saying it's likely that Intellectual Ventures is taking a cut of whatever money Oasis gets from its lawsuits-- Oasis, a company with no operations, no products, and as far as we can tell no employees, whose only activity seems to be taking a very broad patent from 1998 and using it to sue over a dozen internet companies today.
<v Alex Blumberg>所以明确地说，Peter Detkin是在说，Intellectual Ventures很有可能正在从Oasis通过诉讼获得的任何钱财中分成——Oasis，一家没有运营、没有产品，且据我们所知没有员工的公司，其唯一活动似乎是拿出一个1998年的广泛专利，用来起诉当今十几家互联网公司。

00:44:11.080 --> 00:44:19.080
<v Laura Sydell>And so we asked him, how does it feel making money from an entity, which is behaving a lot like the patent trolls that he once condemned?
<v Laura Sydell>所以我们问他，从一个表现得很像他曾经谴责过的专利流氓的实体那里赚钱，感觉如何？

00:44:19.080 --> 00:44:34.620
<v Peter Detkin>These are patents we used to hold, we no longer hold. And we ensure that we have no control over the actions of these third parties. They are independent actors. They're not Intellectual Ventures. They may be monetizing in ways that we disagree with, but it's not our call. It's theirs.
<v Peter Detkin>这些是我们曾经持有的专利，我们现在不再持有了。而且我们确保对这些第三方的行为没有控制权。他们是独立主体。他们不是Intellectual Ventures。他们货币化的方式可能我们不赞同，但这不是我们要决定的。这是他们的事。

00:44:34.620 --> 00:44:35.800
<v Alex Blumberg>You're also still getting paid.
<v Alex Blumberg>你也还在拿钱。

00:44:35.800 --> 00:44:48.070
<v Laura Sydell>Yeah, so I sort of feel like, yeah, well, but what do you expect? You must have some knowledge that it's highly likely these people are going to go and bring lawsuits, especially since they're companies that only have these largely run by attorneys.
<v Laura Sydell>是的，所以我感觉就像，是啊，但你指望什么呢？你肯定知道这些人很有可能去发起诉讼，特别是考虑到它们本身就是主要由律师经营的公司。

00:44:48.070 --> 00:45:05.980
<v Peter Detkin>Sure, I understand. I'm not disputing any of that. What I'm trying to say, and I apologize if I'm not being clear, is that we do believe, we believe in our heart, that litigation is a highly inefficient way to do licensing. But let's not lose sight that litigation is just licensing by other means.
<v Peter Detkin>当然，我明白。我没争辩这些。我想说的是，如果我没表达清楚请见谅，我们确实相信，我们坚信，诉讼是一种效率极低的授权方式。但不要忽视诉讼只是通过其他方式进行的授权。

00:45:05.980 --> 00:45:10.770
<v Alex Blumberg>In other words, we try to license these patents in a friendly way, but sometimes you have to sue.
<v Alex Blumberg>换句话说，我们尝试以友好的方式授权这些专利，但有时你不得不诉讼。

00:45:10.770 --> 00:45:34.190
<v Laura Sydell>Peter Detkin then repeated the company line that we'd heard from a lot of people at IV, that the mission of Intellectual Ventures is to help inventors bring great ideas into the world. That lots of inventors, they're like great artists. Brilliant, but not brilliant at business, so their patents languish. IV gets their ideas into the hands of companies who will actually build what they've invented.
<v Laura Sydell>Peter Detkin接着重复了我们在IV很多人那里听到的公司口号，即Intellectual Ventures的使命是帮助发明家将伟大的想法带到世界上。许多发明家就像伟大的艺术家。才华横溢，但不善经营，所以他们的专利被荒废了。IV将他们的想法交到了真正会制造他们发明的东西的公司手中。

00:45:34.190 --> 00:45:42.390
<v Alex Blumberg>So can you point me to a patent that you acquired that was languishing that then got licensed to somebody and built in a way that I could see?
<v Alex Blumberg>那么你能指出一个你获得的被荒废的专利，然后授权给某人并以我能看到的方式制造出来的例子吗？

00:45:42.390 --> 00:46:29.040
<v Peter Detkin>I can tell you that it's happened, but unfortunately the deal's confidential. There are two deals that were done. One was with a toy company and the other was-- I can't remember the technology of the other one. But they came to us and they said, we're interested in this particular patent. We'd like to take it out into the world. Will you give us a license? We did. And they put it out there. It was out there for last Christmas. I actually don't know how it's done. I'd be curious to find out myself. But I agree, that's an anomaly. I see where you're going with your question and I don't mean to fight you on it. The fact is the bulk of our patents, the bulk of our revenue, is from people using inven-- they were using it before we bought it, and they are using it after we bought it, but we provided an efficient way for them to get access to those invention rights.
<v Peter Detkin>我可以告诉你这发生过，但不幸的是交易是保密的。有两笔交易完成了。一笔是和玩具公司，另一笔是——我不记得另一笔的技术了。但他们找到我们说，我们对这项特定专利感兴趣。我们想把它带到世界上。你能给我们授权吗？我们做了。他们把它推向了市场。去年圣诞节它就在市场上。其实我也没见过它是怎么做的。我本人也很好奇。但我同意，那是个例外。我明白你的问题的意图，我不打算在这上面和你争。事实是，我们的大部分专利，大部分收入，来自人们使用发明的——他们在我们购买之前就在使用它，在我们购买之后他们也在使用它，但我们为他们提供了一种获取这些发明权利的有效途径。

00:46:29.040 --> 00:46:45.790
<v Alex Blumberg>The way I hear what you're saying, the way I translate it in my head is they were using it before without paying a license and nobody was bothering them. And now they are paying a license to you. Why is that a better situation?
<v Alex Blumberg>我听到的你的话，我脑子里的翻译是：他们在付费授权之前就在使用它，没人在打扰他们。而现在他们要向你们支付授权费。为什么那是种更好的情况？

00:46:45.790 --> 00:46:49.040
<v Peter Detkin>Well, because we want to incentivize the guy who invented it.
<v Peter Detkin>好吧，因为我们想激励那个发明它的人。

00:46:49.040 --> 00:47:01.940
<v Alex Blumberg>That is, if companies pay their licenses, inventors make more money, which in turn, gives them incentive to invent more stuff. This is the rhetorical cul-de-sac where every argument with Peter Detkin ends.
<v Alex Blumberg>也就是说，如果公司支付授权费，发明家就能赚更多的钱，进而激励他们发明更多的东西。这就是与Peter Detkin争论的每一条死胡同。

00:47:01.940 --> 00:47:20.430
<v Alex Blumberg>But here's the problem with that argument. IV is not buying inventions. They're buying patents. And as we've heard, as most software engineers will tell you, at least when it comes to computers and the internet, a patent and an invention are not the same. Lots of patents cover things that people in the field wouldn't consider inventions at all.
<v Alex Blumberg>但这个论点的问题在于：IV买的不是发明。他们买的是专利。正如我们所听到的，正如大多数软件工程师会告诉你的，至少在计算机和互联网方面，专利和发明不是一回事。很多专利涵盖的甚至不是该领域的人认为的发明。

00:47:20.430 --> 00:47:38.970
<v Laura Sydell>And these patents out there, that aren't for something novel, or are so broad they can cover anything, every single one of them can be used to bring lawsuits. In response, all the big tech companies have started amassing troves of software patents, not to build anything, but to defend themselves.
<v Laura Sydell>而这些专利，不是针对新颖的东西，或者太宽泛到涵盖任何东西，每一个都可以用来提起诉讼。作为回应，所有的大型科技公司都已经开始积累大量的软件专利，不是为了制造什么，而是为了自卫。

00:47:38.970 --> 00:47:46.630
<v Laura Sydell>If a company's patent hoard is big enough, it can say essentially, if you try to sue me with your patents, I'll sue you with mine.
<v Laura Sydell>如果一家公司的专利库足够大，它基本上可以以此说，如果你试图用你的专利起诉我，我就用我的起诉你。

00:47:46.630 --> 00:48:07.890
<v Alex Blumberg>It's the old mutually assured destruction. Except instead of arsenals of nuclear weapons, it's arsenals of patents. And this was a problem Intellectual Ventures founder Nathan Myhrvold said he was trying to solve when he first started his company, a problem that he and others from Intellectual Ventures talked about in investor meetings all around Silicon Valley. Chris Sacca was there at one of them.
<v Alex Blumberg>这是古老的“相互保证毁灭”。只是不是核武器库，而是专利库。这就是Intellectual Ventures创始人Nathan Myhrvold在刚创办公司时声称试图解决的问题，一个他和Intellectual Ventures其他人曾在硅谷各地的投资者会议上谈论过的问题。Chris Sacca就在其中一个会上。

00:48:07.890 --> 00:48:16.950
<v Chris Sacca>I think I saw Nathan for the first time present the idea of Intellectual Ventures in either the fall of 2007 or the spring of 2008.
<v Chris Sacca>我想我第一次看到Nathan展示Intellectual Ventures的构想是在2007年秋天或2008年春天。

00:48:16.950 --> 00:48:29.890
<v Alex Blumberg>And the pitch he heard was, basically, Intellectual Ventures helps defend against lawsuits. Intellectual Ventures has this hoard of 35,000 patents-- 35,000 patents that, for a price, companies can access and use to defend themselves.
<v Alex Blumberg>他听到的推销基本上是：Intellectual Ventures有助于防范诉讼。Intellectual Ventures拥有这个35000项专利的宝库——35000项专利，只要付钱，公司就可以访问并使用它们来保护自己。

00:48:29.890 --> 00:48:44.170
<v Chris Sacca>They pay administrative fees ranging from the tens of thousands to the millions and millions of dollars, all into this entity to kind of buy themselves insurance that protects them from being sued by any harmful, malevolent outsiders.
<v Chris Sacca>他们支付行政费用，从几万美元到数百万美元不等，全部交给这个实体，就为了给自己买一份保险，保护自己不被任何有害的、恶意的局外人起诉。

00:48:44.170 --> 00:48:57.220
<v Alex Blumberg>In other words, Intellectual Ventures goes around to companies and says, hey, you want to protect yourself from lawsuits? We own tons of patents. Make a deal with us. Our patents will not only cover everything you're doing in your business, no one will dare sue you.
<v Alex Blumberg>换句话说，Intellectual Ventures到处找公司说，嘿，你想保护自己免受诉讼吗？我们有成吨的专利。和我们做个交易。我们的专利不仅会涵盖你在业务中做的所有事情，而且没人敢起诉你。

00:48:57.220 --> 00:49:07.250
<v Laura Sydell>But to Chris Sacca, there's an implication in there. If you don't join us, who knows what will happen? Which reminds him of the business practices of another organization.
<v Laura Sydell>但对Chris Sacca来说，这里面有个暗示。如果你不加入我们，谁知道会发生什么呢？这让他想起了另一个组织的经营方式。

00:49:07.250 --> 00:49:17.320
<v Chris Sacca>A Mafia-style shakedown, where somebody comes in the front door of your building and says, it'd be a shame if this place burned down. I know the neighborhood really well and I can make sure that doesn't happen. And saying, pay us up.
<v Chris Sacca>一种黑手党式的敲诈，有人走进你大楼的前门说，这地方如果烧了就太可惜了。我对这片街区很熟，我可以确保那种事不会发生。并说，付钱给我们。

00:49:17.320 --> 00:50:13.610
<v Chris Sacca>Now here's what's funny. When I've seen Nathan speak publicly about this, and when I've seen spokespeople from Intellectual Ventures, they constantly remind us that they, themselves, don't bring lawsuits. That they, themselves, are not litigators. That they're a defensive player. But the truth is that the threat of their patent arsenal can't actually be realized. It can't be taken seriously unless they have that offensive posture, unless they're willing to assert those patents. And so it's this very delicate balancing act that is quite reminiscent of scenes you see in movies when the Mafia comes to visit your butcher shop. And they say to you, hey, it would be a real shame if somebody else came and sued you. Tell you what-- pay us an exorbitant membership fee into our collective and we'll keep you protected that way. A protection scheme isn't that credible unless some butcher shops burn down now and then.
<v Chris Sacca>现在有趣的是。当我看到Nathan公开谈论这一点时，当我看到Intellectual Ventures的发言人时，他们不断提醒我们，他们自己不提起诉讼。他们自己不是诉讼律师。他们是防御方。但真相是，除非他们有那种进攻姿态，除非他们愿意主张那些专利，否则他们专利库的威胁实际上是无法实现的。它无法被认真对待。所以这是一种非常微妙的平衡行为，相当令人想起黑手党去拜访你的肉铺时的电影场景。他们对你说，嘿，如果别人来起诉你那可就太遗憾了。告诉你——交给我们这笔昂贵的会员费，我们会保护你的。除非时不时真的烧掉一些肉铺，否则这种保护计划就不会那么可信。

00:50:13.610 --> 00:50:46.150
<v Alex Blumberg>We told Intellectual Ventures that Chris Sacca compared their business to a Mafia shakedown. And in an email, Peter Detkin called that "ridiculous and offensive." He then reiterated some of the arguments you've heard about how IV protects inventors, and went on to say quote, "we're a disruptive company that's providing a way for patent holders to recognize value--" recognize value, he means make money-- "that wasn't available before we came on the scene. And we are making a big impact on the market. That obviously makes people uncomfortable, but no amount of name calling changes the fact that ideas have value."
<v Alex Blumberg>我们告诉Intellectual Ventures，Chris Sacca将他们的生意比作黑手党敲诈。在一封电子邮件中，Peter Detkin称这是“荒谬且冒犯性的”。然后他重申了一些你听到过的关于IV如何保护发明家的论点，并继续说，“我们是一家具有破坏性的公司，为专利持有人提供了一种识别价值——识别价值，他的意思是赚钱——的方法，这在我们出现在现场之前是不可用的。我们对市场产生了重大影响。这显然让人不舒服，但无论怎么称呼都无法改变‘创意有价值’这个事实。”

00:50:46.150 --> 00:51:07.860
<v Laura Sydell>True enough, but lately it seems like a lot of butcher shops have been burning. As we were reporting this story, more and more Intellectual Ventures patents started showing up in the hands of companies like Oasis, companies without employees or operations, who were formed for the purpose of filing lawsuits. They're known as non-practicing entities, or NPEs.
<v Laura Sydell>确实如此，但最近似乎很多肉铺都在烧。在我们报道这个故事时，越来越多的Intellectual Ventures专利开始出现在像Oasis这样的公司手中，这些公司没有员工或业务，它们成立的目的就是为了提起诉讼。它们被称为非实施实体（NPE）。

00:51:07.860 --> 00:51:19.690
<v Alex Blumberg>One former IV patent was used by an NPE to sue 19 different companies-- a seemingly random assortment, which included Dell Computers, Abercrombie & Fitch, Visa, UPS.
<v Alex Blumberg>其中一项前IV专利被一家NPE用来起诉19家不同的公司——一个看起来随机的组合，包括戴尔电脑、Abercrombie & Fitch、Visa、UPS。

00:51:19.690 --> 00:51:30.170
<v Alex Blumberg>What's the suit about? These companies all have websites that when you scroll your mouse over certain sections, pop-up boxes appear. This NPE said, we have a patent on that.
<v Alex Blumberg>诉讼是关于什么的？这些公司都有网站，当你的鼠标悬停在某些部分时，会出现弹出框。这家NPE说，我们拥有那个专利。

00:51:30.170 --> 00:51:49.580
<v Laura Sydell>Another group of former IV patents is being used in one of the most controversial and most talked-about cases in Silicon Valley right now. An NPE called Lodsys is suing almost three dozen companies and counting. These are small- and medium-sized companies developing apps for iPhone and Android smartphones.
<v Laura Sydell>另一组前IV专利正在硅谷目前最具争议和讨论最多的案件之一中使用。一家叫Lodsys的NPE正在起诉近三打公司，而且数量还在增加。这些是为iPhone和Android智能手机开发应用程序的中小型公司。

00:51:49.580 --> 00:51:55.650
<v Laura Sydell>Lodsys says every time you buy something within a smartphone app, they own the patent on that.
<v Laura Sydell>Lodsys说，你每次在智能手机应用内购买东西，他们都拥有该专利。

00:51:55.650 --> 00:52:15.830
<v Alex Blumberg>And one interesting wrinkle about that case, the address of Lodsys, 104 East Houston Street, Marshall, Texas, Suite 190-- the same exact address, down to the suite number, as Oasis Research.
<v Alex Blumberg>那个案件中有一个有趣的转折点，Lodsys的地址是德克萨斯州马歇尔市东休斯顿街104号190室——这与Oasis Research的地址完全相同，精确到室号。

00:52:15.830 --> 00:52:23.310
<v Laura Sydell>Tom Ewing, the lawyer who keeps track of Intellectual Ventures, says that all this behavior has led people to come up with a special name for the company.
<v Laura Sydell>追踪Intellectual Ventures的律师Tom Ewing表示，所有这些行为导致人们为该公司取了一个特别的名字。

00:52:23.310 --> 00:52:26.370
<v Tom Ewing>The troll on steroids.
<v Tom Ewing>打了类固醇的流氓。

00:52:26.370 --> 00:52:34.020
<v Laura Sydell>Do you think it's a troll on steroids?
<v Laura Sydell>你觉得它是打了类固醇的流氓吗？

00:52:34.020 --> 00:53:13.710
<v Tom Ewing>I don't want to complicate things, but I personally think there's a whole lot of gray. For example, they've already collected $2 billion worth of royalties, so they say. And you have to ask yourself, of the $2 billion in royalties they've collected, how much of those royalties that they've collected are based on reasonable licensing fees that the people they received it from should have paid? And how much of it is simply based on trying to avoid litigation? And I would say it's probably a mix of both.
<v Tom Ewing>我不想把事情复杂化，但我个人认为这里有很大的灰色地带。例如，据他们所说，他们已经收到了价值20亿美元的特许权使用费。你必须问自己，在他们收到的20亿美元特许权使用费中，有多少是基于收到费用的人本应支付的合理授权费？又有多少纯粹是基于试图避免诉讼？我想这可能是两者的混合。

00:53:13.710 --> 00:53:36.710
<v Laura Sydell>The problem is to try and figure out what that mix actually looks like is virtually impossible. We called dozens of people. We called people who had licensing arrangements with Intellectual Ventures. We called people who were defendants in lawsuits involving Intellectual Ventures patents. We called every single company being sued by Oasis Research, all 16 of them. No one would talk to us.
<v Laura Sydell>问题在于，试图找出这种混合到底是什么样子几乎是不可能的。我们打了几十个电话。我们给与Intellectual Ventures有授权安排的人打了电话。我们给涉及Intellectual Ventures专利诉讼的被告打了电话。我们给Oasis Research起诉的所有16家公司打了电话。没人愿意跟我们谈。

00:53:36.710 --> 00:53:51.650
<v Laura Sydell>Partly this is fear. Partly Intellectual Ventures is said to have the strictest non-disclosure agreement in Silicon Valley.
<v Laura Sydell>部分是因为恐惧。部分是因为据说Intellectual Ventures拥有硅谷最严格的保密协议。

00:53:51.650 --> 00:54:13.570
<v Alex Blumberg>The Oasis Research case is still ongoing, but many of the original defendants seem to have settled. Michael Smith, the attorney in Marshall, Texas, who showed us the Oasis offices, represented one of those defendants. He was pretty sure they would have won the case if they'd gone to trial, but his client settled anyway. He says, sometimes it makes more sense to settle and pay a license fee than to spend $2 to $5 million on a court case.
<v Alex Blumberg>Oasis Research案仍在审理中，但许多原始被告似乎已经和解了。向我们展示Oasis办公室的德克萨斯州马歇尔市律师Michael Smith代表了其中一位被告。他非常确定如果他们进入审判阶段就会赢，但他的客户还是和解了。他说，有时和解并支付授权费比在法庭案件上花费200万到500万美元更有意义。

00:54:13.570 --> 00:54:34.090
<v Laura Sydell>Tom Ewing, the lawyer who tracks Intellectual Ventures, says it's likely we're going to see plenty more of these cases in the future. And that's based just on the math of IV's business model. In order to purchase its 35,000 patents, Intellectual Ventures got money from investors-- a lot of money, more than $5 billion.
<v Laura Sydell>追踪Intellectual Ventures的律师Tom Ewing表示，我们未来很可能会看到更多这样的案件。这仅基于IV商业模式的数学运算。为了购买其35000项专利，Intellectual Ventures从投资者那里获得了资金——一大笔钱，超过50亿美元。

00:54:34.090 --> 00:54:55.720
<v Alex Blumberg>And a lot of these investors are venture capitalists who expect very high returns. These are people who are looking for the next Google, the next Apple, people who want to get back many times what they put in. Since its founding in 2000, Intellectual Ventures has generated $2 billion in revenue. But to keep its investors happy, over the next 10 years, says Tom Ewing, they're going to have to do a lot better than that.
<v Alex Blumberg>而这些投资者中有很多是期望高回报的风险投资人。这些人正在寻找下一个谷歌、下一个苹果，希望获得数倍于投入的回报。自2000年成立以来，Intellectual Ventures产生了20亿美元的收入。但为了让投资者满意，Tom Ewing表示，在接下来的10年里，他们需要做得比这好得多。

00:54:55.720 --> 00:55:15.990
<v Tom Ewing>So if you calculate this out, that means that over, say, a 10-year period, they are going to need to collect about $35 billion in licensing revenue. Which in order for them to be successful among the people who they're trying to compare themselves with.
<v Tom Ewing>所以如果你计算一下，这意味着在比如10年期间，他们将需要收集大约350亿美元的授权收入。为了在他们试图与之比较的人群中取得成功。

00:55:15.990 --> 00:55:31.500
<v Tom Ewing>IV seems to have signed a number of deals. If the stream of deals they're signing doesn't increase significantly, then I would imagine they will be forced to file more litigations in order to achieve their revenue targets.
<v Tom Ewing>IV似乎已经签署了一些交易。如果他们签署的交易量没有显著增加，那么我想他们将被迫提起更多的诉讼以达到他们的收入目标。

00:55:31.500 --> 00:55:51.040
<v Laura Sydell>Tom's prediction already seems to be coming true. Earlier this month, Intellectual Ventures itself filed a patent infringement suit in federal court against several companies it claimed were infringing some patents it owns.
<v Laura Sydell>Tom的预测似乎已经成真了。本月早些时候，Intellectual Ventures本身在联邦法院对几家它声称侵犯了其所拥有专利的公司提起了专利侵权诉讼。

00:55:51.040 --> 00:56:35.920
<v Laura Sydell>In early July, the bankrupt tech company Nortel put its 6,000 patents up for auction as part of a liquidation. A bidding war broke out between the Silicon Valley powerhouses. Google said in press accounts that it wanted the patents purely to defend itself against lawsuits, and it was willing to spend over $3 billion to get them. But that wasn't enough. The portfolio eventually sold to Apple and a strange consortium of other tech companies, including Apple competitor Microsoft. The price tag? $4.5 billion, five times the opening bid. More than double what most people were expecting. The largest patent auction in history.
<v Laura Sydell>7月初，破产的科技公司北电网络（Nortel）将其6000项专利作为清算的一部分进行拍卖。硅谷巨头之间爆发了竞购战。谷歌在媒体报道中表示，它购买这些专利纯粹是为了保护自己免受诉讼，并愿意花费超过30亿美元来获得它们。但这还不够。这些专利组合最终卖给了苹果和一个由其他科技公司组成的奇怪财团，其中包括苹果的竞争对手微软。价格标签是多少？45亿美元，是开标价的五倍。是大多数人预期价格的两倍多。史上最大规模的专利拍卖。

00:56:35.920 --> 00:57:02.240
<v Alex Blumberg>Think of that. $4.5 billion on patents that these companies almost certainly don't want for their technical secrets. That $4.5 billion won't build anything new, won't bring new products to the shelves, won't open up new factories that can hire people who need jobs. That's $4.5 billion that adds to the price of every product these companies sell you, $4.5 billion essentially wasted buying arms for an ongoing patent war.
<v Alex Blumberg>想想看。45亿美元用于那些这些公司几乎肯定不是为了其技术秘密而想要的专利。那45亿美元不会创造任何新东西，不会给货架带来新产品，不会开设能雇佣急需工作的人的新工厂。那是45亿美元，增加了这些公司卖给你的每件产品的价格，那45亿美元基本上浪费在为一场正在进行的专利战争购买武器上。

00:57:02.240 --> 00:57:21.050
<v Alex Blumberg>The big companies-- Google, Apple, Microsoft-- will probably survive this war. The likely casualties, the companies out there that no one's ever heard of that could one day take their place.
<v Alex Blumberg>大公司——谷歌、苹果、微软——可能会在这场战争中幸存下来。可能的伤亡者，是那些无人知晓但有一天可能取而代之的公司。

00:57:21.050 --> 00:57:34.490
<v Ira Glass>Alex Blumberg with NPR correspondent Laura Sydell. Alex is part of our Planet Money team. On the Planet Money website you can find links to all kinds of documents and patent-related goodies, plus, of course, their podcast. That is at npr.org/money.
<v Ira Glass>Alex Blumberg与NPR记者Laura Sydell。Alex是我们Planet Money团队的一员。在Planet Money网站上，你可以找到各种文件和专利相关好物的链接，当然还有他们的播客。网址是npr.org/money。

